From the Carleman inequality we obtain
\[
\|e^{\tau\phi_R}\chi f\|_{L^q(\mathbb R^d)}
\le C\|e^{\tau\phi_R}H_{A,V}(\chi f)\|_{L^p(\mathbb R^d)}.
\]
Note that $\operatorname{supp}\chi\subset\{x\colon r_1\le|x|\le r_3\}$.
Since $f=0$ for $a<|x|<b$, we have
\[
\operatorname{supp}(\chi f)\subset\{x\colon b\le|x|\le r_3\}.
\]
Moreover,
\[
H_{A,V}(\chi f)=\chi H_{A,V}f + [H_{A,V},\chi]f = [H_{A,V},\chi]f.
\]
Hence $\operatorname{supp}\,H_{A,V}(\chi f)\subset\{x\colon r_2<|x|<r_3\}$, and we get
\[
\|e^{\tau\phi_R}f\|_{L^q(S_1)}
\le C\bigl\|e^{\tau\phi_R}[H_{A,V},\chi]f\bigr\|_{L^p(S_2)}.
\]
Recall that $\phi_R=\frac14\bigl(\log\frac{|x|}{R}\bigr)^2$, which is decreasing as $|x|$ increases when $0<|x|<R$.
Take $R>r_3$. Then
\[
e^{\tau\phi_R(b+\delta)}\|f\|_{L^q(S_1)}
\le C\,e^{\tau\phi_R(r_2)}\bigl\|[H_{A,V},\chi]f\bigr\|_{L^p(S_2)}.
\]
By hypothesis $[H_{A,V},\chi]f\in L^p(S_2)$. Letting $\tau\to\infty$,
\[
\|f\|_{L^q(S_1)}=0 \implies f=0 \quad\text{a.e. in }S_1.
\]
This proves $f\equiv 0$ on $b<|x|<b+\delta$.

