By the same argument as before, we obtain
\[
\|e^{\tau\phi_R} f\|_{L^q(S_3)}
\le C\bigl\|e^{\tau\phi_R}[H_{A,V},\chi]f\bigr\|_{L^p(S_4)}.
\]
Recall that $\phi_R=\frac14\bigl(\log\frac{|x|}{R}\bigr)^2$.
Take $R<r_6$. Then $\phi_R(x)$ is decreasing as $|x|$ decreases for $|x|>R$. Consequently,
\[
e^{\tau\phi_R(a-\delta)}\|f\|_{L^q(S_3)}
\le C\,e^{\tau\phi_R(r_5)}\bigl\|[H_{A,V},\chi]f\bigr\|_{L^p(S_4)}.
\]
Letting $\tau\to\infty$, we get $\|f\|_{L^q(S_3)}=0$, hence $f=0$ a.e.\ in $S_3$.
This proves $f=0$ on $a-\delta<|x|<b$.

This means that the vanishing condition $f=0$ on $D_{a,b}$ can be extended to arbitrary annuli.

We now prove that if $f=0$ a.e.\ on every annulus $0<\frac{1}{R}<|x|<R<+\infty$, then $f=0$ a.e.\ in $\mathbb R^d$.

Set
\[
E \overset{\mathrm{def}}{=} \bigl\{x\in\mathbb R^d\colon f(x)\neq 0\bigr\}.
\]
Suppose for contradiction that $m(E)\neq 0$. Then
\[
0<m(E)\le m\bigl(E\cap B_{\frac1R}\bigr)
+ m\bigl(E\cap (B_{\frac1R}^c\cap B_R)\bigr)
+ m\bigl(E\cap B_R^c\bigr).
\]

Since $f=0$ a.e.\ on every annulus $0<\frac{1}{R}<|x|<R<+\infty$, we have
\[
m\bigl(E\cap (B_{\frac1R}^c\cap B_R)\bigr)=0 \quad \text{for all } R>0.
\]

For sufficiently large $R$,
\[
m\bigl(E\cap B_{\frac1R}\bigr)<\frac{m(E)}{2},
\]
which implies
\[
m\bigl(E\cap B_R^c\bigr)\ge \frac{m(E)}{2}.
\]

Observe that
\[
\begin{aligned}
m\bigl(E\cap B_R^c\bigr)
&= m\left(E\cap \bigcup_{j=R}^{\infty}\big(B_{j+1}\setminus B_j\big)\right)\\
&= m\left(\bigcup_{j=R}^{\infty} \Big(E\cap \big(B_{j+1}\setminus B_j\big)\Big)\right).
\end{aligned}
\]

There must exist some $j_0$ such that $m\big(E\cap (B_{j_0+1}\setminus B_{j_0})\big)>0$.
If not, then
\[
m\left(\bigcup_{j=R}^{\infty} \Big(E\cap \big(B_{j+1}\setminus B_j\big)\Big)\right)
\le \sum_{j=R}^{\infty} m\big(E\cap (B_{j+1}\setminus B_j)\big)=0.
\]

This contradicts
\[
m\left(\bigcup_{j=R}^{\infty} \Big(E\cap \big(B_{j+1}\setminus B_j\big)\Big)\right)\ge \frac{m(E)}{2}.
\]

However, $m\big(E\cap (B_{j_0+1}\setminus B_{j_0})\big)>0$ contradicts our hypothesis.
Hence $m(E)=0$, which completes the proof.
